          %h1#the_category_of_sets The category of sets
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#characterization"} Characterization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#size"} Size
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OppositeCategory"} Opposite category and Boolean algebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#endoadjunctions"} Endo-adjunctions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_categories"} Related categories
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is the (or a)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            between sets as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
          %p
            This definition is somewhat vague by design. Rather than canonize a fixed set of principles, the nLab adopts a ‘pluralist’ point of view which recognizes different needs and foundational assumptions among mathematicians who use set theory. Thus, there are various axes to consider when formulating categorical properties one thinks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            should satisfy, including
          %ul
            %li
              %p first-order vs. higher-order logic
            %li
              %p
                impredicative vs.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            %li
              %p ‘variability’ vs. ‘constancy’ (Lawvere)
            %li
              %p classical logic vs. intuitionistic logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image+factorizations"} image factorizations
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+sets"} quotient sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                vs
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
                structures, i.e.
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+construction+of+free+algebras"} transfinite construction of free algebras
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+inductive+types"} quotient inductive types
            %li
              %p choice principles
            %li
              %p replacement or collection principles
            %li
              %p smallness structures
          %p just to name a few. Quite a bit of axiomatic fine-tuning can enter when one considers the panoply of hypotheses that might appeal to one or another school of intuitionism or constructivism, or various combinatorial or cardinal hypotheses one might attach to ZFC, etc.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#characterization Characterization
          %p
            The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            has many marvelous properties, which make it a common choice for serving as a ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"}> foundation
            ’ of mathematics. For instance:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                It is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+category"} well-pointed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %p
                So in particular it is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            %li
              %p
                It is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small"} locally small
            %li
              %p
                It is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
                (in fact,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    locally finitely presentable
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/locally+finitely+presentable"} ?
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive
                (as is any cocomplete topos).
          %p
            At least assuming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            uniquely up to equivalence among all categories; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+well-pointed+topos"} cocomplete well-pointed topos
            %em small
            and therefore a knowledge of what a
            %em set
            (as opposed to a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core+groupoid"} core groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            (whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijections"} bijections
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Core
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Core(Set)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object+classifier"} discrete object classifier
              in the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd
              that the set of truth values
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              does in classifying subsets in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:I \rightarrow Set
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+family"} indexed family
              of sets and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is an index groupoid.
          %p
            As a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is also characterized by the fact that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
              and
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\colon E \to Set
              from any other topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
          %p
            It is usually assumed that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            and has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is asserted to be a well-pointed topos that satisfies the axiom of choice and has a natural numbers object. It follows that it is automatically “locally small” and “complete and cocomplete” relative to the notion of “smallness” defined in terms of itself (actually, this is true for any topos).
          %p
            Conversely,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            cannot satisfy the axiom of choice (since this implies
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            (that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            has
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} enough projectives
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
            is not even a topos, although most predicativists would still agree that it is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            pretopos.
          %h3#size Size
          %p
            Above we considered
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            to be the category of
            %em all
            sets, so that in particular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            itself is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            as part of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            often define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            to be the category of
            %em small
            sets (those contained in the universe). One often then writes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SET
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SET
            for the category of
            %em large
            sets, which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+enlargement"} universe enlargement
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h3#OppositeCategory Opposite category and Boolean algebras
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Bool
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{P} \;\colon\; Set \to Bool^{op}
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
              which in its
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/%28eso%2Bfull%2C+faithful%29+factorization+system"} (eso+full, faithful) factorization
              induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              of that of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+atomic+Boolean+algebras"} complete atomic Boolean algebras
          %p
            See for instance
            %a{:href => "#vanOosten"} van Oosten, theorem 2.4
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Restricted to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
              this equivalence restricts to an equivalence with finite Boolean algebras. See at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
              for more on this.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In constructive mathematics,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
              defines an equivalence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
              with the opposite category of that of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom"} atomic
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebras"} Heyting algebras
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power object
              functor defines an equivalence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              with the opposite category of that of internal complete atomic Heyting algebras. (This phrase can be interpreted using the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
              of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %h3#endoadjunctions Endo-adjunctions
          %p
            Left adjoint endofunctors on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            are cocontinuous, and so are uniquely defined on the terminal generator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:Set \to Set
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1) = S
            for a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is uniquely the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(X) = S \times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(X) = S \to X
            given by currying.
